module extdeps.filesystem.rust_realization

// THE RUST REALIZATION OF create-new PUBLISH, AS ONE AUTHORITY BOTH CONSUMERS COPY.
//
// `extdeps.filesystem` owns the INTERFACE shape of a filesystem operation. This module owns one
// REALIZATION of it -- the Rust transport handler bound to that shape -- which DESIGN section 3
// keeps peripheral to the interface rather than inside it. It is a separate module for the reason
// the section gives: interface, realization and policy are three facts, not one row.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. `Filesystem.WriteCreateNew` had TWO Rust realizations. The seed interpreter
// carried a hand-written `write_file_create_new`, and `v1.compiler.emit_rust` carried a string of
// Rust source emitted into every compiled program. One fact, two authorities -- and review
// 5089156132 on gunbc#10069 measured the consequence: finding 3 repaired the interpreter to
// stage-then-publish while the emitted spelling still opened the target directly, so a failed
// emitted write left a partial repository that the model reported as never created. Repairing the
// reachable authority left the other lying, which is DESIGN section 4b rung honesty: a class's rung
// is the MINIMUM across its paths, so a fix on one path raises nothing.
//
// #10069 brought the two spellings back into agreement BY HAND, and said so in its own annotation:
// nothing but review held them in step. This module is the construction that replaces that review.
//
// THE SEAM IS A COMPLETE NAMED FUNCTION, NOT A BLOCK, AND THAT CHOICE IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.
//
// The obvious repair -- keep the emitted bare block and teach the seed to build a function from it
// -- was proposed and REFUSED on external review, correctly. That translator would have to know
// what the block assumes in scope and how its result channel maps onto `io::Result`, which makes it
// a THIRD hand-maintained authority sitting on the seam. It would be worse than the fork it
// replaces: a fork fails loudly the moment the two sides disagree, while a translator keeps
// producing something plausible from inputs it has misunderstood.
//
// So the row below is a whole function definition, and BOTH consumers copy these exact bytes. The
// emitted crate places it in its lib.rs root beside the other shared definitions and reaches it
// through `use crate::`; the seed receives the same text as a committed generated artifact and
// calls it. Nothing transforms the text on either path, so there is no seam left to maintain into
// disagreement.
//
// IT RETURNS `io::Result<()>`, NOT THE FILE RESULT TUPLE, and that is not seed ergonomics. The
// `(success, content, error, byte_count)` tuple is the RESULT-CHANNEL POLICY of the File transport:
// a stringified error and a byte count. Making it canonical would stringify `std::io::Error` inside
// the realization and discard the host's structured failure before any consumer asked for that
// loss -- policy fused into realization, the layer inversion DESIGN section 3 names. Each consumer
// projects the outcome into whatever channel it owns; that projection is ordinary consumer code,
// not a second implementation.
//
// THE STAGING NAME IS ALLOCATED, NOT CALCULATED, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE REPAIR.
//
// #10069 shipped `{path}.gunbc-create-{pid}`: unique per TARGET, but not per THREAD. Two threads of
// one process creating the same target derived the same name, so the loser failed its exclusive
// open on the TEMPORARY and reported AlreadyExists against an internal name it had never asked to
// write. Refusing was right -- one of two racers must lose -- but the refusal did not locate itself
// at the operator's subject, and if the winner then failed too, both calls could refuse with no
// target ever published.
//
// A PER-ATTEMPT SEQUENCE ALONE DOES NOT CLOSE THAT CLASS, which external review established after
// the first version of this module tried exactly that. A sequence makes the name unique among LIVE
// calls in one process; it does not make an occupied candidate unobservable. This function
// deliberately ignores removal failure after publication, so stale staging files are an ADMITTED
// physical state, and a later process may reuse the pid and restart its sequence at zero. A one-shot
// candidate that returns its own AlreadyExists therefore reproduces the identical defect -- target
// absent, legitimate create refused because an INTERNAL filename was occupied -- at a lower
// frequency, which is worse than the original for being harder to observe.
//
// So the loop ALLOCATES: an occupied candidate is skipped and the next one tried; any other open
// error is the host's and is returned unchanged; exhaustion is CHECKED and refuses with a typed
// cause rather than wrapping around onto a sequence it has already used. Crucially the occupied arm
// does not remove the file it found -- that file belongs to another attempt, and deleting it would
// convert a naming collision into data loss.
data rust_file_write_create_new_fn_def: String = "fn gunbc_file_write_create_new(file_path: &str, content: &[u8]) -> std::io::Result<()> {\n    use std::io::Write;\n    static GUNBC_CREATE_SEQ: std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64 = std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::new(0);\n    const GUNBC_CREATE_MAX_CANDIDATES: u32 = 1024;\n    let mut attempted: u32 = 0;\n    let (mut staged, staging_path) = loop {\n        if attempted >= GUNBC_CREATE_MAX_CANDIDATES {\n            return Err(std::io::Error::new(\n                std::io::ErrorKind::AlreadyExists,\n                \"gunbc create-new: exhausted staging candidates without acquiring one\",\n            ));\n        }\n        attempted += 1;\n        let seq = GUNBC_CREATE_SEQ.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed);\n        let candidate = format!(\"{}.gunbc-create-{}-{}\", file_path, std::process::id(), seq);\n        match std::fs::OpenOptions::new()\n            .write(true)\n            .create_new(true)\n            .open(&candidate)\n        {\n            Ok(file) => break (file, candidate),\n            Err(occupied) if occupied.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::AlreadyExists => continue,\n            Err(host) => return Err(host),\n        }\n    };\n    if let Err(staging_err) = staged.write_all(content) {\n        let _ = std::fs::remove_file(&staging_path);\n        return Err(staging_err);\n    }\n    if let Err(sync_err) = staged.sync_all() {\n        let _ = std::fs::remove_file(&staging_path);\n        return Err(sync_err);\n    }\n    drop(staged);\n    let published = std::fs::hard_link(&staging_path, file_path);\n    let _ = std::fs::remove_file(&staging_path);\n    published\n}\n"

// THE SEED'S COPY, AS A COMMITTED GENERATED ARTIFACT.
//
// The emitted crate receives the definition through its own lib.rs root. The seed cannot: it is
// ordinary Rust compiled by cargo, and it cannot execute a String. So it receives the SAME BYTES as
// a generated .rs module that the stage0 crate compiles and calls, enrolled in
// gunbc.generated_artifact like every other committed projection -- which means the regeneration
// and fixed-point gates refuse drift on it rather than a reviewer noticing.
//
// THE HEADER IS THE ORDINARY GENERATED-ARTIFACT HEADER, so the merge driver and the drift gate
// treat this file exactly as they treat the others. `pub` because the interpreter is a different
// module of the same crate; the emitted crate's copy is crate-root and needs no qualifier.
fn expected_gunbc_file_transport_generated_rs() -> String {
  join([
    "// Generated by v1 compiler -- do not edit.\n",
    "// Source module: extdeps.filesystem.rust_realization\n",
    "\n",
    "pub ", rust_file_write_create_new_fn_def,
  ], "")
}
